  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nTonight through Saturday...Northwest flow aloft persists across the \nregion as ridging aloft remains wedged into the Lower Mississippi \nRiver Valley from the west.  Meanwhile at the surface, high pressure \ncentered over the Great Lakes region extends southward into the CWA, \nresulting in northeast winds across the area.  This'll continue to \npromote quiet weather across the CWA through the middle of the week. \nHigh temperatures will gradually warm a few degrees each day across \nthe area, but through mid-week the dry airmass currently over the \nregion will remain present under easterly flow. Despite the warming \nafternoon high temperatures, this dry air will allow for less humid \nconditions during the day, and pleasant conditions during the \nevening and overnight hours.\n\nAs we move into the latter portion of the week and into the weekend, \nmoisture will subtly increase across the region as southerly flow at \nthe surface and aloft returns.  In turn, rain chances will also \nbegin increasing each day, mainly Thursday into Saturday, as the \naforementioned ridge aloft slides east of the Lower Mississippi \nRiver Valley during week's end.  Increased troughing to the west, \nand the eventual eastward progression of this trough over the \nweekend, may result in a cold front traversing the CWA late in the\nweekend. Other than the overall increasing moisture & heat \nresulting in better rain chances across the area at the end of the\nforecast period, confidence in a frontal passage during this time\nis currently low. This is due to run-to-run inconsistencies in \nglobal models, as some runs stall the front west of the region and\nnever bring it through the CWA. This scenario would maintain a\nmore moist and warmer airmass across the area, with continued\ndecent rain chances. /19/",
